front:
**Add types to totalPrice**

This JavaScript doesn't compile under `strict`: its parameters are implicitly `any`. Add types — items are `{ price: number; quantity: number }` — so it compiles and rejects bad calls.

```ts
function totalPrice(items, taxRate) {
  const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);
  return Math.round(subtotal * (1 + taxRate) * 100) / 100;
}
```

back:
// Why: Parameters are where types matter most: inside the function TypeScript infers the
// rest (`sum`, `item`, `subtotal`). Typing them `any` compiles but checks nothing.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/basic-types.html
type LineItem = { price: number; quantity: number };

function totalPrice(items: LineItem[], taxRate: number): number {
  const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);
  return Math.round(subtotal * (1 + taxRate) * 100) / 100;
}

